The relative saving appears to survive: the small-conductor transfer costs only a fixed power of \(\log x\), and the stated \(K\) leaves room for both large-sieve bounds. I found a separate issue in the first-moment application: \(\beta_\ell\) includes small moduli outside the interval covered by the invoked dyadic bounds. Adding the filter already satisfied by the fibres repairs that step.

